\title{X1 - few bits of reference count and in-place reuse in the hash-consed store}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{store}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/profiles/X1.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Scout report, 2026-11-24. Subject: the owner's idea to borrow from Reussir the few-bit reference count (in the limit one bit, unique / shared) that licenses in-place reuse - "may not be a good idea; use whatever good specialization / JIT trick you can think of". Method: a census of every record and run the machine makes (who made it, was it ever found again, who holds it, is it dead when the judgment that made it returns), four candidate schemes judged against it, prototypes of what the census points to, counters first, then time. Living summary: \code{\verb~|work/status/prof-X1.md~}; working files: \code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/~}. Everything here is the JVM launcher with ONE worker, on the main tree of \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~} as it was at 15:09Z (the tree has moved since: section 5.3).}

\subtree{
\title{0. Verdict in one page}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The bit of Reussir has nothing to bite on in this store, and the census says why.} The cases it specialises - extend the spine just built, extend or prune the frame just built, bump a Nat offset cell - are 1.9-7.2 \% of the records, 0.4-3.4 \% of the new frames and a few hundred cells per corpus; working environments are already a LIFO slab (1 copy in 162 M pushes over the nine census runs); and where the case occurs the source is held by a memo word or an environment (by nothing in 0.0 \% of the cases in v0). Rewriting a hash-consed record in place trades an insert for a delete: it saves bytes, not a probe. Schemes (a) fresh-top rewrite, (c) 2-bit header counts: \strong{no-go}; (d) builders: \strong{already the design}; (b) region rollback: \strong{no-go as a speed-up}. None of these four was built; the verdicts rest on the census.}
\li{\strong{What IS unshared is the memo key, not the value.} The largest class of records everywhere is the tabling record of EVAL - 25 \% of all records on the Cedar slice, 35 \% on Init, 43 \% on reachability, still 22 / 30 / 39 \% under \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} - and 82-99.5 \% of them are never found again; each also cost a PRUNE and often a frame. On reachability 97.5 \% of ALL records are never found again.}
\li{\strong{So the one bit goes on the KEY} (\code{\verb~|once~}, 117 lines in \code{\verb~|Eval.java~}): one bit of visit count per tabling key in a Bloom filter outside the store; the first evaluation of a key makes no frame and no record, the second is tabled as today, so a key is evaluated at most twice. Counters against v0: records -18 \% (Cedar slice), -30 \% (Init), -28 \% (Std), -29 \% (con-leche), -41 \% (reachability: 22.9 M -> 13.5 M); ticks +5.8 \% / +5.4 \% / +5.7 \% / +5.3 \% / +0.7 \%; peak RSS of the JVM on reachability 3,052 -> 1,533 MiB. \strong{Warm check time (\src{bench/quiet.py}, last pass of a process): reachability -27 \% against v0 (5,687 -> 4,143 ms; cold first pass -23 \%) and -29 \% against \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} (5,585 -> 3,972 ms).} Init under \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}: about -6 \% (one process, below all three baseline processes). Cedar slice, and Init against v0: no resolved change (-1.3 \%, -1.0 \%; the samples overlap). The gain is where sessions are large; where they are small it is memory only.}
\li{\strong{The second idea did not survive the clock.} \code{\verb~|evc~}, an ephemeral EVAL memo with the key inline in front of the filter, does in counters exactly what it was built for (alone: every counter identical to v0; with \code{\verb~|once~}: ticks +1.0 \% / +0.6 \% / +0.2 \% instead of +5.8 \% / +5.4 \% / +0.7 \%; under \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} 2-11 \% FEWER ticks than the base) - and in time it is never ahead of \code{\verb~|once~} alone: +7.9 \% alone on the Cedar slice, 6.7 \% and 10.4 \% slower than \code{\verb~|once~} alone on reachability (v0, s1). Ticks are not time. \strong{No-go as built}; so is the per-node two-bit counter \code{\verb~|prof~}.}
\li{\strong{Correct on everything it was run on}: the machine's accepted / rejected / declined counts are equal with the switches off and on in all 28 counter runs of six corpora; per declaration against the reference engine (M8's harness): no SOUNDNESS and no COMPLETENESS line in any report - tutorial 395 / 395 units agree, bugs 1,427 of 1,428, the Cedar slice 8,407 of 8,418, reachability 4,405 of 4,413, Init 58,383 of 58,547 (\code{\verb~|evc + once~}), and every decline is the base tree's own eager-reduction decline. \code{\verb~|once~} alone: tutorial and bugs on the measured tree, and tutorial, bugs, the Cedar slice and reachability on the patched main tree of 16:53Z (section 5.3).}
\li{\strong{Hand-over}: \code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/patches/0001-eval-visit-filter.main-1653Z.patch~}, one file, owner M2. Not established: 8 workers, the native image, steady state beyond three passes, the large corpora (section 7).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\p{\strong{Trees.} \code{\verb~|scripts/machine-sandbox.sh init X1~} refuses ("work package 'X1' owns nothing in OWNERS.tsv"; \code{\verb~|test~} and \code{\verb~|pull~} likewise), and claiming a file would edit the main tree. So: a copy of the main tree of \code{\verb~|rlvm-machine~} taken under the sandbox lock at 15:09:21Z (after S's publication of 15:08:59Z; the last code publication in it is M6's of 15:07:19Z) at \code{\verb~|work/sandbox/X1/rlvm-machine~}, a pristine twin \code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/base1507/rlvm-machine~} for the diffs, both built with the command that \code{\verb~|test~} runs (\code{\verb~|$MVN -o -q -f .../pom.xml -Drlvm.root=$PWD~}). Nothing was published.}

\p{\strong{Census build} (\code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/census/~}): the base tree plus 54 exact-string hooks (\code{\verb~|instrument.py~}; every hook must match exactly once, so a source that moved fails loudly) and one class \code{\verb~|Shadow.java~}. No value, memo word, tick or table entry differs from the uninstrumented machine (the counters \code{\verb~|records~}, \code{\verb~|runWords~}, \code{\verb~|ticks~}, \code{\verb~|pairs~}, accepted and declined of the statistics file are identical in both builds in the 14 census runs, of 15, that have an uninstrumented twin). Shadow arrays indexed by record id and run id hold: who made it (the EVAL site, or "other" = INFER / CONV / reduction), the event at which it was made, the number of later intern probes that found it and the event of the last one, the event at which the EVAL judgment that made it completed. One event = a new record, a new run, a probe hit or a completed EVAL node; ticks are sampled every 64 events for the lifetime histograms. At every session end (\code{\verb~|Machine.reset~}, \code{\verb~|MachineSession.close~}) the session is scanned once:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{found again}: at least one later intern probe returned the record (the benefit of hash-consing);}
\li{\strong{E0}: never found again and referenced by nothing at the end of the session (no slot, field or memo word);}
\li{\strong{TE} (transitively ephemeral): never found again, and every referrer is itself TE (a reference-count cascade from the E0 records): no later probe ever reached it or anything that holds it;}
\li{\strong{dead at the return of the judgment that made it}: the record was made between \code{\verb~|Eval.node~} and \code{\verb~|Eval.done~} of an EVAL node (innermost; a tabling record belongs to the evaluation it tables), it is not that judgment's result, and after the judgment completed it was neither found by a probe nor stored into a newer record or run, nor is it reachable (fields, slots, memo words) from a record that is live in this sense. Records made outside any EVAL node (by INFER, CONV, the reduction judgments) have no such return event; for them the tables give "never live again" (never found and not reachable from a live record). Reads through a Java local are invisible to the census, so "dead" is an upper bound on what a region could release, and "result" counts as live however short it lives;}
\li{\strong{copy logs}: every NEU made by extending another NEU, every NSUC made from an NSUC, every new frame with the frame whose ENTER started the environment it was pruned from; classified at the session end (was the source the newest record or run; was it ever touched again; is it TE).}
}

\p{\strong{Runs.} JVM launcher, one worker, 6 GB heap, one host slot per batch; the machine as primary engine with no switch (\strong{v0}: every non-leaf node tabled, one session per declaration) and with \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~} (\strong{s1}, the candidate default of the integration log; session cap 4 MiB). Corpora: \code{\verb~|init-prelude~} (smoke), Init, Std, the Cedar slice \code{\verb~|cedar-hot-ax~}, reachability, and con-leche as the additional project corpus. In all of them the machine as copied declines the declarations that enter eager reduction (M6's \code{\verb~|erDeclines~} of 15:07Z; the statistics count 22 declines on the Cedar slice = 11 units, 328 on Init = 164 units, 16 on reachability = 8 units, 406 on Std, 176 on con-leche); the reference engine checks those. Raw data: \code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/census/data/*.tsv~} (+ the statistics file of each run); per-run tables: \code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/census/tables/~}.}

\p{\strong{Prototype counters} come from the uninstrumented sandbox jar in the same private distribution, switch on / off by a system property in ONE binary (\code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/ab/counters.tsv~}); the census build of the sandbox (\code{\verb~|census-proto~}) gives the probe counts. \strong{Timings} were taken only through \src{bench/quiet.py} (section 5.4).}
}

