\title{3. Reading the census}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Hash-consing pays for values and for closures, not for the tabling keys.} Probes that hit: 30-52 \% overall, and they are concentrated: LAM closures are found again 30 \% of the time on Init and the Cedar slice and absorb 63-66 \% of their probes as hits; INFER's tabling records are found again 39-70 \%. The tabling records of EVAL are the largest class of all (22-43 \% of the records) and 82-99.5 \% of them are never found again; each of them cost a PRUNE (probe of \code{\verb~|rtab~}, 30-90 \% of the time a new frame), a probe of \code{\verb~|vtab~}, eight words of record and a VAL store.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|gated~} does not remove this class on the workloads that reduce.} On Init it gates 40 \% of the \code{\verb~|Eval.table~} calls out; on the Cedar slice 32 \% and on reachability 27 \%, and what remains is found again as rarely as before (C3). One reason is in the gate's own rule: every node of an EARLIER unit's term (an unfolded definition body) is "reached from outside its segment" and is always tabled. On reachability s1 executes more ticks than v0 (35.40 M against 34.57 M).}
\li{\strong{Reachability is a different regime.} 2.5 \% of ALL records are ever found again; four declarations make 93 \% of the records (sessions of 2.7 to 8 million records, intern tables of 64-256 MiB: M1's profile has 39 \% of the process waiting for intern-table slots there). NEU 35 \% of the records, 2.9 \% found again; LAM 14 \%, 1.3 \% found again but 5.9 M hits on that 1.3 \% (a few hot closures, hit 2,048 ticks or more after they were made). Interning finds little there outside a small hot set.}
\li{\strong{Where sessions are large, hits are long-distance.} Of the probe hits on SUSP and FRAME records, those that come 2,048 ticks or more after the record was made are 30-37 \% on Init and Std (small sessions), 47 \% on con-leche, 56-64 \% on the Cedar slice and 73-87 \% on reachability (C7): a small "young" intern table in front of the big one would miss most hits exactly where the big one is expensive. (The second visit of a KEY, when there is one, mostly comes soon - the cache-size sweep of section 5.2 - but that did not turn into time.)}
\li{\strong{The reuse cases of reussir are rare here, and for a reason.} Spines are built n-ary from scratch (extending an existing NEU makes 1.9-7.2 \% of the records, and in v0 its source is always held by a memo word or an environment); frames are pruned from the working environment, never extended; environments are a LIFO slab (1 copy in 162 M pushes over all runs); Nat offsets are immediates. What reussir reuses in place - the constructor cell consumed by a match - is here a hash-consed NEU whose rewrite is already "in place" in the only sense the store allows: its memo words (\code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|UNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}), the thunk update of ISA section 10.}
\li{\strong{Frames are sparse where environments are deep}: 75 \% of the frame slots on the Cedar slice are HOLE (18 slots per frame, 87 \% of the run words), 35 \% on Init, 23 \% on reachability. This is M1's open item (sparse frames), not a uniqueness question, but it is 54 M of the 92 M run words of the slice.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The schemes, their invariants, what breaks them, what the census says}

\p{Vocabulary of the store, as it is today (\code{\verb~|Values~}, \code{\verb~|Runs~}, \code{\verb~|Machine~}): every record is in \code{\verb~|vtab~}, every FRAME / REST run in \code{\verb~|rtab~}, from the moment it exists (\code{\verb~|addRecordAt~} / \code{\verb~|putRunAt~} in the miss branch of the probe); ids are positions in two bump slabs; nothing is ever removed before the session reset, which un-inserts every record and run by re-probing. Ids are held by: spine and frame slots, the fields \code{\verb~|a~} of NEU / PROJ / NSUC / FVAR, the memo words \code{\verb~|m0..m3~} of every kind (\code{\verb~|VAL~}, \code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|TYPE~}, \code{\verb~|UNF~}, \code{\verb~|DOM~}, \code{\verb~|COD~}, the scope words of FVAR), the pair table and its log, the direct-mapped caches (\code{\verb~|CHEAD~}, sort cache, literal import, \code{\verb~|FV0~}, \code{\verb~|SIGT~}, \code{\verb~|IC~}, \code{\verb~|UNDEC~}, the closed-term memo, the APPLY memo, the REST chain cache), the OPEN bit table (indexed by id), the scope stack, the worklist payloads, scratch \code{\verb~|X~}, the environment slab \code{\verb~|es~}, the register stack, the rule trees of \code{\verb~|Rules~}, and Java locals of every judgment on the host stack. A record header has no free bit (kind 4 + flags 12 + aux 16); a value ref has two reserved bits (\code{\verb~|REF_EPH~}, \code{\verb~|REF_UNQ~}).}

\subtree{
\title{(a) One bit "fresh and unpublished" on the newest record: rewrite it in place before it is interned}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Mechanism} (designed, not built). The record at \code{\verb~|vp - 1~} (and, for a NEU, its spine at the end of \code{\verb~|rs~}) is rewritten instead of copied: its \code{\verb~|vtab~} slot is zeroed, the words are rewritten, the new key is probed; if the new key exists the fresh record is popped (\code{\verb~|vp--~}, \code{\verb~|rsp~} back), else it is entered again. Zeroing the slot of the NEWEST record is a valid deletion in an open-addressing table without tombstones: no entry was inserted after it, so no probe sequence passes over its slot. This also holds after a growth, because \code{\verb~|growVtab~} / \code{\verb~|growRtab~} re-insert in id order.}
\li{\strong{Invariants.} F1: the id is held by nothing but the caller's operand (no slot, memo word, pair entry, cache cell, frame payload, scratch word, other Java local). F2: it is the newest record and its run the newest run. F3: its entry in the OPEN bit table is rewritten with it.}
\li{\strong{What breaks F1.} In v0 everything (census: the source of a spine extension is E0, i.e. held by nothing, in 0.0 \% of the cases). Where the prefix is the result of the body of a beta step that is applied further, \code{\verb~|done~} has just stored it as \code{\verb~|VAL~} of the body's tabling record; where it is the value of a variable, the environment holds it. It is unpublished only where the body is not memoised (\code{\verb~|m2a~}'s untabled bodies), and then the APPLY memo (\code{\verb~|m2b~}), the ephemeral memo of (e) or any other memo of the body publishes it again. Java locals cannot be audited: one missed holder is a value that changes under its reader, i.e. a wrong ACCEPT.}
\li{\strong{Census (C5).} Spine extension of an existing NEU: 1.2 \% of the NEU probes on init-prelude, 5.5-11 \% on the Cedar slice, Init, Std and con-leche, 17-20 \% on reachability; the new records are 1.9 \% / 3.0 \% / 4.2 \% / 7.2 \% of ALL records (Cedar slice / Init / con-leche / reachability, v0); the source is the newest record in 65-73 \% of them. Frames: a new frame whose entered source frame is never touched again, only reachable from never-found records, contained in the new frame and the newest run: 3.4 \% of new frames on the Cedar slice, 1.0 \% on con-leche, 0.5 \% on Init, 0.4 \% on reachability. NSUC made from NSUC: 6 to 878 per corpus. \code{\verb~|PUSH~} copies: 1 in 162 M pushes over all runs.}
\li{\strong{What it would save.} At best (source newest, never touched again, only reachable from never-found records) 1.3 \% (Cedar slice), 2.0 \% (Init), 4.7 \% (reachability) of the records and the copy of a spine of 4 words; no probe (the delete replaces the insert of the prefix, the insert of the result stays), so no time is to be expected from it (argued, not prototyped).}
\li{\strong{Verdict: NO-GO.} The case is rare, the saving is memory only, and the precondition is false wherever the body is memoised.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{(b) An ephemeral region with mark / release per judgment, interning only on escape}

\p{Three different things hide under this name.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{(b1) Region rollback of records and runs} (designed, not built). At a mark \code{\verb~|(vp, rsp, plogN)~}; at the return of the judgment every record and run above the mark that the result does not reach is dropped. Deleting ALL entries above a mark from \code{\verb~|vtab~} / \code{\verb~|rtab~} is valid for the same reason as in (a) (insertion order is id order, also after a growth), so no tombstones are needed; the cost is one probe per dropped entry, i.e. what the session reset pays today. Invariants: R1 no id above the mark survives: the result is below the mark or is copied down and re-interned; R2 every memo word of an OLDER record that was written during the judgment and points above the mark is undone: this needs a write barrier and an undo log at every memo store of every package (\code{\verb~|VAL~}, \code{\verb~|WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|CORE~}, \code{\verb~|TYPE~}, \code{\verb~|UNF~}, \code{\verb~|DOM~}, \code{\verb~|COD~}, FVAR scope words, PROOF / PROPT bits are safe); R3 the pair entries logged since the mark are removed (\code{\verb~|plog~} is in insertion order: same argument); R4 every direct-mapped cache is invalidated (\code{\verb~|epoch++~}), the OPEN bit table is cut, NatStore ids made above the mark are released; R5 no suspended frame, scratch word or register holds an id above the mark (true at a judgment's return by I-STACKS, false inside a \code{\verb~|TRY~} that is cut). Census: of the records made inside an EVAL judgment 43 \% (Cedar v0), 47 \% (reachability v0) are dead when that judgment returns (C1: 25-43 \% of all records) - but they are almost exactly the tabling records and their frames (C3), which \code{\verb~|once~} of (e) below never makes. Releasing costs what making cost (one probe each); what it buys is a smaller working set in the giant sessions (reachability: 4 declarations make 93 \% of the records). \strong{Verdict: NO-GO as a speed-up; keep as the v2 answer to a memory wall} (ISA section 10 already says so), and only around judgments whose result is a boolean (\code{\verb~|CONV~}, \code{\verb~|PIRR~}, a \code{\verb~|TRY~} segment), where R1 is free.}
\li{\strong{(b2) Ephemeral memo keys.} The KEY of a memo entry - the bindings a node reads - is the only thing that is interned for the sake of the memo alone (a frame and a SUSP record per evaluated node). Not interning it for a first evaluation, and in a second form holding it inline in a lossy cache cell until it would otherwise be lost, keeps every result exact: this is scheme (e), the one that was built.}
\li{\strong{(b3) Non-interned frames / closures / spines (\code{\verb~|REF_EPH~}).} A closure over a frame that is in no table, a NEU that is in no table. Breaks I-ID: two constructions of the same value get two ids. Conversion stays sound only if no judgment ever answers "different" from two different ids (by the ISA's own forms it must not today: a SUSP and its VAL, an NSUC and the literal it folds to are two ids of one value; I did not audit \code{\verb~|Conv~} for it), but every identity-keyed memo loses its hits on such values (pair table, \code{\verb~|WHNF~} / \code{\verb~|UNF~} memo words live in the record that is not found again, tabling keys that contain the id), the reset and the table growth must know which records are not in the table (the reset loop scans the whole table for a missing entry), and the FVAR scope protocol keys its variable by the type's id. Census: this is where the remaining volume is - on reachability NEU records are 35 \% of all records and 2.9 \% of them are ever found again, LAM closures 14 \% and 1.3 \%; on Init NEU 32 \% and 15 \%, on the Cedar slice 19 \% and 20 \%. \strong{Verdict: v2 experiment, not prototyped here.} It is the real "gruszka trade" (ISA section 10, last row), it needs its own soundness review, and its yardstick is reachability-like closed evaluation only.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{(c) Saturating 2-bit counts in the record header}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Mechanism.} Count the stores of an id: slot of a new run, field of a new record, memo word, pair entry, cache cell; a probe hit counts as a store (a second holder). 0 = held by Java locals only, 1 = one owner, 2+ = shared.}
\li{\strong{Invariants.} C1: every store increments before the id becomes reachable through it (one load and one store into a count table per slot written; in the record header it would be the random header load that M1 removed from PRUNE: 17.5 \% of the worker on Init); C2: the count never decrements (no owner ever releases), so "1" means "was stored once", not "is unique now"; C3: Java locals are not counted, so "0" licenses nothing either.}
\li{\strong{What it could license.} In-place extension when the count is 0 (as (a), without the "newest" condition, so with a real table deletion); skipping a memo for unshared values (the official kernel's \code{\verb~|is_shared~} gating) - but here the memo words are in the record and cost nothing to keep, and the memo that does cost (the tabling record) is not keyed by a value at all.}
\li{\strong{Census.} E0 ("never found again, never stored": the count stays 0) is 36-44 \% of the records, of which 75 \% (Cedar slice) to 92 \% (Init) are tabling records of EVAL and INFER (in the table on purpose, referenced by nothing by construction); for NEU it is 0.0 \%, for LAM 1-8 \%. No constructor has a population of count-0 operands to specialise on.}
\li{\strong{Verdict: NO-GO.} Nothing to specialise, a write on every store, and no free header bits (a side table like the OPEN bits would be needed).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{(d) Builders: assemble in registers / scratch, intern once}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Already the design, and the census confirms it works:} spines are collected in \code{\verb~|X~} and interned with their NEU in one probe (the spine of an application node is never built argument by argument: \code{\verb~|PUSH~} copies 0, spine extensions are the application of a value that is already a NEU, 2-7 \% of the records); frames are pruned from the working environment in one probe, without building the frame first (stage A); environments are a LIFO slab, extended in place; Nat literals are immediates and NSUC cells are tens to hundreds per corpus.}
\li{\strong{Remaining intermediates} (C2, C5): (1) the prefix NEU of an application that is extended (a closure whose body returns a neutral and is applied further, or a neutral variable applied): 1.9-7.2 \% of the records. A builder form exists for the first (hand the pending arguments to the MKAPP that ends the body), but the prefix is the value of the body and every memo of the body stores it: it can only be skipped for unmemoised bodies. (2) the tabling record and its frame per evaluated node: 22-43 \% of the records: scheme (e). (3) PII ("the type of this lambda"): 6 \% of the records on the Cedar slice, 1-2 \% found again; \code{\verb~|expect~} (M5) is the builder answer and removes 18 \% of them on the slice. (4) FVAR per opened binder type: 5 \% on the Cedar slice.}
\li{\strong{Verdict: nothing new to build under this heading}; (1) is NO-GO for now (small, and in conflict with (e)'s memo).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{(e) What the census points to instead: one bit per memo KEY, and an ephemeral memo in front of it}

\p{The bit of reussir answers "is this cell shared?" so that an unshared cell is rewritten instead of copied. In a hash-consed store the cell is shared by definition; what is unshared 82-99 \% of the time is the memo KEY. So:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|once~}: one bit of visit count per tabling key}, kept outside the store in a blocked Bloom filter (two bits of one 64-bit word per key, at most 8 keys per word, rebuilt at twice the size from a log of the entered hashes; back to 64 words at the session reset). \code{\verb~|Eval.table~} asks the filter: a key that was never entered is evaluated WITHOUT a tabling record - no PRUNE probe, no frame, no SUSP record, no VAL store (the path that \code{\verb~|gated~} already uses for untabled nodes) - and is entered; a key that was entered is tabled as today. Invariants: O1 equal keys have equal hashes (the hash reads the term, ls' and the bindings at the mask's positions: the tabling key of ISA 5.1); O2 an entered key stays entered until the session reset (the log); O3 the filter decides only WHETHER a memo is kept, never a value: a false "seen" tables at the first visit (today's behaviour), a key that was tabled through a false "seen" and reads "not seen" later is evaluated once more. Consequence: a key is evaluated at most twice where the table evaluates it once, and a shared node of a DAG is evaluated at most twice per environment - the bound for which static gating needed its depth rule (M2's finding of 14:55Z: 44,030 against 8,038,031 ticks on a dependent nest before that rule).}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|evc~}: an ephemeral EVAL memo.} Direct-mapped cells of 16 words \code{\verb~|(term, ls', epoch, -, value, up to 11 kept bindings)~}: the tabling key is held INLINE and compared exactly, so a hit returns what \code{\verb~|VAL~} of the tabling record would be, with one hash over the kept bindings and one cache line: no PRUNE, no probe of \code{\verb~|rtab~} / \code{\verb~|vtab~}. Every evaluation that completes on the host stack is written into its cell (\code{\verb~|Eval.node~}). With \code{\verb~|once~} behind it, a key gets a frame and a record only when it comes back AFTER its cell was overwritten: interning on escape, where "escape" is "outlived the cache". Invariants: E1 a cell matches only on the whole key (term, ls', epoch, every kept binding; a node with more than 11 kept bindings or a far mask is not cached); E2 the value is never a SUSP (as for \code{\verb~|VAL~}, 2.6); E3 cells die with the session (epoch stamp), like the closed-term memo; E4 the bindings are read from the entry environment, which is intact when the node returns (I-STACKS; the put is skipped for a judgment that was suspended); E5 off under every debug mode that owns the hit path, and to be off in strict mode. What it changes that is observable: ticks, the memo contents, and the representation of some values up to PEEK (a suspension where the table had its VAL) - the same class of change as \code{\verb~|gated~}; verdicts and reduction results are unchanged (section 5.3).}
\li{\code{\verb~|prof~} (a two-bit saturating counter per term node that sends nodes whose keys repeat back to immediate tabling) was built as the fall-back for the case that \code{\verb~|once~} alone re-evaluates too much.}
}

\p{Section 5 measures the three: \code{\verb~|once~} is the go; \code{\verb~|evc~} does in counters what is said here and does not pay in time (5.4); \code{\verb~|prof~} is not needed (5.2).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Prototypes}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 What was built}

\p{All of it is in ONE file of the sandbox, \code{\verb~|Eval.java~} (owner M2), 298 added and 3 changed lines, behind bits of the measurement property that file already has (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.m2.x~}), so one binary gives before and after and the default is unchanged (same ticks, records, run words and pairs as the base tree on every corpus). The design is scheme (e) of section 4:}

\table{
\tr{\th{bit}\th{name}\th{what it does}\th{where}}
\tr{\td{512}\td{\code{\verb~|evc~}}\td{the ephemeral EVAL memo: 2^13 direct-mapped cells of 16 words (512 KiB per worker; \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.x1.evc=N~} for 2^N), key inline, looked up in \code{\verb~|eval~} / \code{\verb~|evalInAt~} after the inline answers and before \code{\verb~|table~}; written in \code{\verb~|node~} when the evaluation completed on the host stack}\td{\code{\verb~|evcGet~}, \code{\verb~|evcPut~}, \code{\verb~|keyHash~}}}
\tr{\td{128}\td{\code{\verb~|once~}}\td{the visit filter in \code{\verb~|table~}: a key that was never entered is evaluated untabled (returns \code{\verb~|-t~}, the path \code{\verb~|gated~} uses)}\td{\code{\verb~|firstVisit~}, \code{\verb~|onceGrow~}}}
\tr{\td{256}\td{\code{\verb~|prof~}}\td{with \code{\verb~|once~}: a two-bit saturating counter per term node (2^16 direct-mapped bytes, never reset) sends nodes whose keys repeat back to immediate tabling}\td{in \code{\verb~|table~}}}
}

\p{The first version of the filter was wrong in a way worth recording: it was enlarged by copying its bits into both halves, which keeps every entered key but also keeps the SHARE of set bits, so in a large session half or more of the answers were false "seen" (93 \% in a test with 8 M keys) and the filter silently degraded to today's behaviour. It showed as "new records at a second visit that are never found again" in the census of reachability. The fixed filter is rebuilt from a log of the entered hashes (3.3 \% false "seen" with 4 M random keys); every number below is from the fixed one.}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 Counters (one binary, switch off / on; JVM, one worker; \code{\verb~|work/prof/X1/ab/counters.tsv~})}

\p{Deltas are against the row \code{\verb~|v0~} (or \code{\verb~|s1~}) of the same corpus. "EVAL memo hits" counts the hits of the tabling records and of the ephemeral memo together.}

