\tr{\td{reachability}\td{s1 + once}\td{35.57 M (+0.5\%)}\td{12.87 M (-37.7\%)}\td{77.26 M (-39.4\%)}\td{51.1 K (-0.2\%)}\td{52 (-1.9\%)}\td{8.40 M (+0.4\%)}\td{644.8 K (-3.0\%)}\td{755}\td{1497}\td{4216 / 16}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{s1 + evc + once}\td{34.54 M (-2.4\%)}\td{12.83 M (-37.9\%)}\td{77.00 M (-39.7\%)}\td{51.0 K (-0.4\%)}\td{51 (-3.8\%)}\td{8.26 M (-1.2\%)}\td{1.54 M (+132.3\%)}\td{753}\td{1485}\td{4216 / 16}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{v0}\td{138.73 M}\td{86.14 M}\td{554.55 M}\td{6.08 M}\td{36.6 K}\td{23.68 M}\td{27.66 M}\td{5349}\td{1234}\td{36186 / 176}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{v0 + once}\td{146.11 M (+5.3\%)}\td{61.41 M (-28.7\%)}\td{408.20 M (-26.4\%)}\td{6.08 M (+0.0\%)}\td{36.6 K (+0.0\%)}\td{26.07 M (+10.1\%)}\td{27.93 M (+1.0\%)}\td{3862}\td{946}\td{36186 / 176}}
}

\p{What the rows say:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|evc~} alone changes no counter at all}: ticks, records, run words, pair entries, EVAL evaluations and memo hits are identical to v0 on the Cedar slice, Init and reachability. Every miss is still tabled, every hit returns the value that \code{\verb~|VAL~} holds; only the cost of a hit changes (section 5.4). It is also a check of its exactness: a wrong value would have to leave every one of these counters where it was.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|once~} alone} removes 18 \% (Cedar slice) to 41 \% (reachability) of the records, but on the INFER-driven corpora it costs 5-6 \% ticks and 10-21 \% more EVAL evaluations: a key that comes a second time is evaluated a second time. On Init 19.4 M keys are left untabled at their first visit, 2.5 M of them (13 \%) come back and are tabled then, and 71 \% of those records are never found again (74 \% on con-leche): a key is visited once, or exactly twice, far more often than three times - consistent with M2's observation that INFER's telescope walk evaluates an argument by its own ARGV and once more inside its parent.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|evc + once~}}: the second visit is answered from the cell, so the records go and the ticks stay: Cedar slice -22 \% records, -16 \% run words, +1.0 \% ticks; Init -33 \%, -36 \%, +0.6 \%; reachability -42 \%, -43 \%, +0.2 \% (22.9 M -> 13.4 M records).}
\li{\strong{Under \code{\verb~|s1~}} the same pair LOWERS the ticks (Cedar slice -6.2 \%, Init -10.8 \%, reachability -2.4 \%) while removing 19-38 \% of the records: the ephemeral memo also answers the nodes that the gate left untabled and that were re-walked every time (memo hits +30 \%, +65 \%, +132 \%). Session resets fall with the footprint (144 -> 136, 1129 -> 1034, 53 -> 51).}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|prof~}} (the per-node counter) does what it was built for - fewer second evaluations (+0.8 \% ticks instead of +1.0 \% on the Cedar slice) - and pays for it with records (-20.4 \% instead of -22.0 \%). With \code{\verb~|evc~} in front there is nothing left for it to fix: not recommended.}
\li{\strong{Cache size} (\code{\verb~|evc + once~}, Cedar slice, against v0): 2^10 cells (64 KiB) ticks +2.2 \%, records -21.1 \%; 2^13 (512 KiB, the default) +1.0 \%, -22.0 \%; 2^16 (4 MiB) +0.2 \%, -22.6 \%. Of the 5.8 \% extra ticks of \code{\verb~|once~} alone, 1,024 cells remove 62 \%, 8,192 cells 83 \%, 65,536 cells 97 \%: the second visit of a key mostly comes soon after the first. The default of 2^13 cells was a guess; it was not tuned by time.}
}

\p{What is left, from the census build of the prototype (same definitions as section 2; Init also with \code{\verb~|once~} alone, the Cedar slice and reachability with \code{\verb~|evc + once~}, which makes 1-6 \% fewer records than \code{\verb~|once~} alone):}

